---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Nav from '../components/Nav.astro';
import Footer from '../components/Footer.astro';
import PageIntro from '../components/PageIntro.astro';
import Section from '../components/Section.astro';
import Cmd from '../components/Cmd.astro';
import Faq from '../components/Faq.astro';
import { faqPick, site } from '../content/site';

const title = 'Codex CLI memory across sessions | hippo';
const description =
  'Hippo adds a block to AGENTS.md and two hooks for Codex, and can capture Codex sessions if you opt in. Memories stay in SQLite on your machine. Our agent test ran on Claude Code only, so there is no measured Codex result.';
const codexMemories = 'https://learn.chatgpt.com/docs/customization/memories';
---

<Base title={title} description={description} crumb="Codex" published="2026-09-28">
  <Nav />
  <main id="main">
    <PageIntro>
      <Fragment slot="title">Codex CLI memory <span class="text-ink">across sessions</span></Fragment>
      <p>
        Codex reads <code>AGENTS.md</code> before it starts work, so that is where <code>hippo init</code> adds its
        block. Lessons from earlier sessions, and from your other agents, then come back when they match the task. Our
        repeat-mistake test ran on Claude Code only, so we have no measured result for Codex.
      </p>
    </PageIntro>

    <Section title="Set it up">
      <Cmd lines={[site.installCmd, 'hippo init', 'hippo hook install codex']} copy />
      <p>
        Init adds two hook entries for Codex, and Codex runs them only after you trust them in <code>/hooks</code>. The
        prompt hook sends up to 5 memories that match each prompt. Capturing sessions is a separate, opt-in step.
        <code>hippo hook install codex</code> wraps the Codex launcher, and <code>hippo hook uninstall codex</code>
        removes the wrapper.
      </p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="What hippo adds to AGENTS.md" alt>
      <p>
        The block sits between <code>&lt;!-- hippo:start --&gt;</code> and <code>&lt;!-- hippo:end --&gt;</code> markers,
        so the rest of your <code>AGENTS.md</code> stays yours. It tells Codex that the prompt hook injects pinned rules
        and matching memories, and to run <code>hippo context --auto --budget 1500</code> only when no memory block has
        appeared in the session.
      </p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="Codex's own memories" id="codex-memories-and-hippo">
      <p>
        Codex has a Memories feature of its own, off by default, that writes local memory files under
        <code>~/.codex/memories/</code>
        (<a class="link" href={codexMemories} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">OpenAI docs<span class="sr-only"> (opens in new tab)</span></a>,
        checked 25 October 2026). When it is on, hippo reads <code>memory_summary.md</code> from that folder at init, at
        every sleep and in the daily run, and copies it into your global store so your other agents can recall it. Hippo
        never writes to Codex's folders. Notes that look like secrets are skipped, and email addresses are masked.
      </p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="What the wrapper does" id="what-the-codex-wrapper-does" alt>
      <p>
        After <code>hippo hook install codex</code>, each launch goes through <code>hippo codex-run</code>, which starts
        the real Codex. When Codex exits, a background worker runs <code>hippo sleep</code> and captures lessons from the
        session transcript into the folder's store, or into the global store when the folder has none.
      </p>
    </Section>

    <Faq
      items={faqPick(['How do I give Codex memory across sessions?'])}
      heading="Codex questions"
      moreHref="/faq/"
    />
  </main>
  <Footer />
</Base>
